00:00:02.253 --> 00:00:05.550
Hi, I'm Pedro.
Thanks for stopping by.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:08.880
I'm a designer from Portugal.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:15.360
I've been a bit of a nerd
about systems, if I say so myself,

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.630
since about 2007, 2008,
first in industrial design

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:24.340
and now in software
and user interface design.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:27.730
I was designing PMM for four years.

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:32.560
It's Percona's tool
for monitoring databases.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:37.900
So when I joined, I knew very
little about that, about databases.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.470
So what I decided
to do was to listen,

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:47.630
as I always do in this line of work.
I talked to DBAs, the user base,

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:52.340
watch them work, and ended
up learning their language.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:55.100
This is what I bring
to technical products.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:01:01.000
I learn how people think,
how users use the products,

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.900
understand their needs,
and I shape products around that,

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:09.940
not the other way around.
And to show ideas the solutions,

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:13.820
I also ended up building
up my skill in code,

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:18.120
making the prototypes myself,
and contributing to production.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.020
Anyway, if you're curious for more,

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:30.020
this here around my website
is a little literal website map

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:34.020
of some of my recent work.
So each triangle is a project.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:39.020
And if you're, yeah,
that's my proposition.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:43.030
If you're building complex tools
and software workflows for people,

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:47.362
let's talk. And yeah,
have a nice one. Thanks. Thanks.

